{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A panel of non-experts following a checklist is more reliable than a single expert because the checklist acts as a 'variance floor', eliminating the catastrophic 'black swan' failures of expert intuition while the panel's collective oversight prevents the 'blind obedience' failure of a single non-expert.","counterconditions":["The task requires 'synthesis' (connecting disparate dots) rather than 'analysis' (checking a list of requirements).","The checklist contains a systemic error that the expert's intuition would have naturally bypassed.","The panel suffers from 'social loafing' where members assume others have verified the checklist items."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The 'Variance Floor' mechanism: Expert intuition is a high-mean but high-variance process (prone to idiosyncratic blind spots). A checklist is a low-mean but ultra-low-variance process. In tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, the panel's role is not to 'solve' the problem but to 'verify the execution' of the checklist, effectively turning the task into a series of binary checks where the probability of collective failure is lower than the probability of a single expert's cognitive lapse.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Genuine reliability gain is distinguished from mere compliance by the presence of 'Non-Linear Temporal Friction'—specifically, a high-variance distribution of time spent per checklist item.","counterconditions":["The panel is under strict time pressure, forcing a linear progression regardless of complexity.","The checklist is so perfectly designed that every item requires exactly the same amount of cognitive effort.","The panel members are inexperienced and spend equal time on every item due to general uncertainty."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Drawing from SCR_003 and SCR_015, mere compliance manifests as a linear progression through the checklist (uniform time-per-item). Genuine reliability manifests as 'cognitive spiking,' where the panel spends disproportionate time on items that are ambiguous or critical for the specific case. This temporal trace provides evidence that the panel is exercising critical judgment (reliability) rather than just marking boxes (compliance).","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel is fundamentally less reliable because the checklist transforms the task from 'problem solving' into 'pattern matching,' making the system fragile to 'out-of-distribution' cases that an expert would instinctively handle.","counterconditions":["The problem domain is strictly bounded and fully captured by the checklist.","The expert's 'open world' intuition is dominated by cognitive biases that lead to consistent errors.","The checklist includes a 'catch-all' item that triggers a referral to an expert for anomalies."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The 'Fragility of Formalization' mechanism: A checklist defines a closed world. Any problem instance that falls outside the checklist's implicit ontology is invisible to the non-expert panel. The expert, relying on a mental model of the domain (intuition), operates in an open world and can detect when the 'rules' no longer apply, whereas the panel will confidently produce a wrong answer by correctly following a misleading list.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Reliability gain is evidenced by 'Corrective Divergence'—the presence of documented internal disagreement on checklist items that eventually converges on the correct answer.","counterconditions":["The panel is subject to strong groupthink, suppressing divergence to maintain harmony.","The checklist is so unambiguous that disagreement is impossible for any competent reader.","The convergence is based on the authority of a single panel member rather than evidence."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Based on SCR_006, if a panel arrives at the right answer with 100% consensus and zero internal debate, it is 'mere compliance' (the checklist did the work). If the panel's logs show initial disagreement (divergence) followed by a reasoned resolution (convergence), it proves the panel acted as a reliability filter, correcting individual misinterpretations of the checklist to reach the truth.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel's reliability is actually a proxy for the checklist author's expertise, making the 'non-expert' status of the panel a feature that prevents 'expert over-correction'.","counterconditions":["The task requires a 'leap of insight' that no checklist can capture.","The checklist is outdated, and the expert's intuition is based on more current data.","The non-experts are so untrained that they misinterpret the basic instructions of the checklist."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The 'Anti-Expertise' mechanism: Experts often 'shortcut' basic steps because they believe they know the outcome (intuitive leaps). Non-experts, lacking this confidence, are more likely to adhere strictly to the foundational steps. In tasks where the 'correct' path is tedious but objective, the non-expert's lack of intuition prevents the 'expert's blind spot' (the assumption that a basic step is unnecessary).","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Genuine reliability is distinguished from compliance by the panel's ability to 'Debug the Tool'—specifically, flagging a checklist item as inapplicable or misleading for the specific case while still reaching the correct outcome.","counterconditions":["The checklist is mathematically optimal 